`linkQUICStream` embeds both `*quic.Conn` and `*quic.Stream`, so `Close()` resolves to `quic.Stream.Close()`, which only closes the send side of the stream. The connection itself stays open, and with `KeepAlivePeriod` set it never reaches `MaxIdleTimeout`. Each time a QUIC peer is disconnected and dialled again, the old connection and its UDP socket stay open and keep sending keepalives.

Also in `linkQUIC.dial`, if `OpenStreamSync` fails, `qc` is not closed.

To reproduce: start a node listening on `quic://127.0.0.1:<port>`, then on a second node call `AddPeer` / `RemovePeer` for it a few times. The number of UDP sockets held by the second node grows by one each time.

Fix:

```diff
--- a/src/core/link_quic.go
+++ b/src/core/link_quic.go
@@ -24,6 +24,10 @@ type linkQUICStream struct {
        *quic.Stream
 }

+func (s *linkQUICStream) Close() error {
+       return s.Conn.CloseWithError(1, "link closed")
+}
+
 type linkQUICListener struct {
        *quic.Listener
        ch <-chan *linkQUICStream
@@ -68,6 +72,7 @@ func (l *linkQUIC) dial(ctx context.Context, url *url.URL, info linkInfo, option
                }
                qs, err := qc.OpenStreamSync(ctx)
                if err != nil {
+                       _ = qc.CloseWithError(1, "stream error")
                        return nil, err
                }
                return &linkQUICStream{
