Concurrent reads can duplicate one buffered byte and drop the next.

I tested `a47ea87d1a03a45d658c1b7a85a4dfbb2cf94dbf` on Linux with Go 1.27.4. The original pipelined `hello` reproduction passes, but two readers can receive `AA` from the buffered prefix `AB`, with no error reporting that `A` was duplicated and `B` was lost.

`connection.Read` reads and advances `remainData` without synchronization. `stat.Connection` embeds [`net.Conn`](https://pkg.go.dev/net#Conn), whose methods may be invoked concurrently.

Minimal reproduction—add this file under `transport/internet/httpupgrade`:

```go
package httpupgrade

import (
	"net"
	"sort"
	"testing"
)

func TestConnectionConcurrentReadsPreservePrefix(t *testing.T) {
	for iteration := 0; iteration < 10000; iteration++ {
		underlying, peer := net.Pipe()
		_ = peer.Close()
		conn := newConnection(underlying, underlying.RemoteAddr(), []byte("AB"))

		start := make(chan struct{})
		type result struct {
			b   byte
			n   int
			err error
		}
		results := make(chan result, 2)
		readOne := func() {
			<-start
			buf := []byte{0}
			n, err := conn.Read(buf)
			results <- result{b: buf[0], n: n, err: err}
		}

		go readOne()
		go readOne()
		close(start)

		got := []byte{}
		for range 2 {
			r := <-results
			if r.err != nil || r.n != 1 {
				t.Fatalf("iteration %d: read returned n=%d err=%v", iteration, r.n, r.err)
			}
			got = append(got, r.b)
		}
		_ = conn.Close()

		sort.Slice(got, func(i, j int) bool { return got[i] < got[j] })
		if string(got) != "AB" {
			t.Fatalf("iteration %d: got %q, want each prefix byte exactly once", iteration, got)
		}
	}
}
```

Run it with:

```sh
go test -race ./transport/internet/httpupgrade \
  -run '^TestConnectionConcurrentReadsPreservePrefix$' -count=1 -v

go test ./transport/internet/httpupgrade \
  -run '^TestConnectionConcurrentReadsPreservePrefix$' -count=20 -v
```

The first command reports a race between the read at `connection.go:24` and the slice update at line 26. Without `-race`, 5 of 20 runs returned `AA`.

Could the prefix check, copy, and cursor update be protected by a read mutex, with the mutex released before falling through to the underlying `Conn.Read`? I tested that small prototype: the original pipelined-payload test still passed, and this test passed 20/20 times under `-race`.

I used AI assistance to review the stream handoff and prepare the test, then ran and verified the reproduction locally in an isolated Linux environment.

